  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a voltage-driven structural evolution of a MnO–LiF composite into a new mixed surface phase. On the left, the starting material is shown as MnO with dispersed LiF particles. An arrow labeled with activation above 4.5 V indicates that high-voltage treatment converts this composite into a particle with an MnO core and an altered outer region described as “MnO–F”-like, suggesting fluorine becomes incorporated at the surface rather than remaining as separate LiF.\n\nThe central and right parts emphasize the crystal-structure change associated with this surface layer. A rock-salt arrangement is shown transforming into a spinel-like arrangement through a “diffusionless transformation,” with Li+ and an electron indicated as participating reversibly. This implies that lithium insertion and removal can switch the structure without large-scale atomic diffusion, likely preserving the framework while changing cation ordering.\n\nOverall, the image conveys a mechanism in which electrochemical activation creates a fluorinated MnO surface and enables a reversible rock-salt to spinel-like conversion during redox. The key message is that the activated surface structure, not just the original MnO–LiF mixture, appears central to the material’s electrochemical behavior.",
